We will prove the following stronger statement   $$ (xy+yz+zx)\left(\frac{1}{(y+z)^2}+
\frac{1}{(z+x)^2}+\frac{1}{(x+y)^2}\right)\ge \frac{9}{4} $$   where  $x, y, z$  are nonnegative reals such that  $x+y, y+z, z+x\ne 0$ .
This will imply the problem because \begin{align*}
\left (1+ \frac{x}{y+z} \right )^2+\left (1+ \frac{y}{z+x} \right )^2+\left (1+ \frac{z}{x+y} \right )^2 &= (x+y+z)^2\left(\frac{1}{(y+z)^2}+
\frac{1}{(z+x)^2}+\frac{1}{(x+y)^2}\right) 
&\ge 3(xy+yz+zx)\left(\frac{1}{(y+z)^2}+
\frac{1}{(z+x)^2}+\frac{1}{(x+y)^2}\right) 
&\ge \frac{27}{4}
\end{align*}
with equality if (because obviously  $3\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^2=\frac{27}{4}$ ) and only if  $x=y=z$  (because  $(x+y+z)^2\ge 3(xy+yz+zx)$ ).

The proof of the proposed stronger statement goes as follows. Let  $a=y+z$ ,  $b=z+x$ ,  $c=x+y$ , and suppose without loss of generality that  $a\ge b\ge c$ . Then some boring expansion gives that
\begin{align*}
(xy+yz+zx)\left(\frac{1}{(y+z)^2}+
\frac{1}{(z+x)^2}+\frac{1}{(x+y)^2}\right)&\ge \frac{9}{4} 
\iff b^2c^2(2ab+2bc+2ca-a^2-b^2-c^2)&\ge 9a^2b^2c^2
\end{align*}
Note that \begin{align*}
\iff b^2c^2(2ab+2bc+2ca-a^2-b^2-c^2) - 9a^2b^2c^2 &= -\sum_{cyc}b^2c^2(b-c)^2+2\sum_{cyc}a^2bc(b-c)^2 
&= \sum_{cyc} bc(2a^2-bc)(b-c)^2
\end{align*}
In what follows let  $S_a=bc(2a^2-bc)$  and define  $S_b$ ,  $S_c$  otherwise. Then we can see that
\begin{align*}
b^2S_b + c^2S_c &= abc(2b^3-abc+2c^3-abc) 
&= 2abc(b^3+c^3-abc) 
&\ge 0
\end{align*}
so  $b^2S_b+c^2S_c\ge 0$ .
Note that it suffices to show that  $S_b\left(\frac{a-c}{a-b}\right)^2+S_c\ge 0$ , since we will then have \begin{align*}
\sum_{cyc}S_a(b-c)^2 &\ge (a-b)^2\left(S_b\left(\frac{a-c}{a-b}\right)^2+S_c\right) 
&\ge 0
\end{align*}. Since  $\frac{a-c}{a-b}=1+\frac{b-c}{a-b}$  is a decreasing function, the inequality is sharpest at  $a=b+c$  (by the fact that  $a, b, c$  are the side lengths of a triangle) which obviously follows from  $b^2S_b+c^2S_c\ge 0$ .

