solution_md:
We use complex numbers; set  $O = AC \cap BD$  as the origin. In general, the projection of the complex number  $z$  onto the line through  $p$  and  $q$  is \[\frac{(\overline{p} - \overline{q})z + (p - q)\overline{z} + \overline{p}q - p\overline{q}}{2(\overline{p} - \overline{q})}\] Then the projection of  $O$  onto  $AB$  is \[p_{AB} = \frac{\overline{a}b - a\overline{b}}{2(\overline{a} - \overline{b})}\] and similarly for the other sides. These projections are cyclic iff \[\frac{(p_{BC} - p_{AB})(p_{CD} - p_{DA})}{(p_{CD} - p_{AB})(p_{BC} - p_{DA})} = \frac{(\overline{p_{BC}} - \overline{p_{AB}})(\overline{p_{CD}} - \overline{p_{DA}})}{(\overline{p_{CD}} - \overline{p_{AB}})(\overline{p_{BC}} - \overline{p_{DA}})}\] We can simplify \begin{align*} \frac{p_{BC} -p_{AB}}{\overline{p_{BC}} - \overline{p_{AB}}} = \frac{\frac{\overline{b}c - b\overline{c}}{2(\overline{b} - \overline{c})} - \frac{\overline{a}b - a\overline{b}}{2(\overline{a} - \overline{b})}}{\frac{b\overline{c}- \overline{b}c}{2(b - c)} - \frac{a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b}{2(a - b)}} &=  \frac{\overline{b}(a - b)(b - c) (a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b + b\overline{c} - \overline{b}c + c\overline{a} - \overline{c}a)}{-b(\overline{a} - \overline{b})(\overline{b} - \overline{c}) (a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b + b\overline{c} - \overline{b}c + c\overline{a} - \overline{c}a)} = \frac{\overline{b}(a - b)(b - c)}{-b(\overline{a} - \overline{b})(\overline{b} - \overline{c})} \end{align*}

Similarly, \[\frac{p_{CD} -p_{DA}}{\overline{p_{CD}} - \overline{p_{DA}}} = \frac{\overline{d}(a - d)(d - c)}{-d(\overline{a} - \overline{d})(\overline{d} - \overline{c})}\]

Next \begin{align*} \frac{p_{CD} -p_{AB}}{\overline{p_{CD}} - \overline{p_{AB}}} = \frac{\frac{\overline{c}d - c\overline{d}}{2(\overline{c} - \overline{d})} - \frac{\overline{a}b - a\overline{b}}{2(\overline{a} - \overline{b})}}{\frac{c\overline{d}- \overline{c}d}{2(c - d)} - \frac{a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b}{2(a - b)}} &= \frac{(a - b)(c - d)[(\overline{c}d - c\overline{d})(\overline{a} - \overline{b}) - (\overline{a}b - a\overline{b})(\overline{c} - \overline{d})]}{(\overline{a} - \overline{b})(\overline{c} -\overline{d})[(c\overline{d} - \overline{c}d)(a - b) - (a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b)(c - d)]} \end{align*} 

Analogously, \[\frac{p_{BC} -p_{DA}}{\overline{p_{BC}} - \overline{p_{DA}}} = \frac{(a - d)(c - b)[(\overline{c}b - c\overline{b})(\overline{a} - \overline{d}) - (\overline{a}d - a\overline{d})(\overline{c} - \overline{b})]}{(\overline{a} - \overline{d})(\overline{c} -\overline{b})[(c\overline{b} - \overline{c}b)(a - d) - (a\overline{d} - \overline{a}d)(c - b)]}\] Multiplying pairwise and cancelling gives \[\frac{\overline{bd}[(c\overline{d} - \overline{c}d)(a - b) - (a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b)(c - d)][(c\overline{b} - \overline{c}b)(a - d) - (a\overline{d} - \overline{a}d)(c - b)]}{bd[(\overline{c}d - c\overline{d})(\overline{a} - \overline{b}) - (\overline{a}b - a\overline{b})(\overline{c} - \overline{d})][(c\overline{b} - \overline{c}b)(\overline{a} - \overline{d}) - (a\overline{d} - \overline{a}d)(c - b)]} = 1\]

But  $O = AC \cap BD$ , so there are constants  $p$  and  $q$  with  $c = pa$  and  $d = qb$ . Then the above can be rewritten \[\frac{q\overline{b}^2(a\overline{b} - \overline{a}b)^2(pqa - pqb - pa + qb)(-pqa - pqb + pa + qb)}{qb^2(\overline{a}b - a\overline{b})^2(pq\overline{a} - pq\overline{b} - p\overline{a} + q\overline{b})(- pq\overline{a} - pq\overline{b} + p\overline{a} + q\overline{b})} = 1\] Simplifying, \[{\frac{\overline{b}^2(pqa - pqb - pa + qb)(-pqa - pqb + pa + qb)}{b^2(pq\overline{a} - pq\overline{b} - p\overline{a} + q\overline{b})(- pq\overline{a} - pq\overline{b} + p\overline{a} + q\overline{b})}} = 1\] Cross multiplying and expanding (which is actually pretty reasonable), we can factor the resulting expression as \[(\overline{a}b - a\overline{b})(\overline{a}b + a\overline{b})p^2(q - 1)^2\] Now  $q = 1$  would mean  $B \equiv D$ , which is degenerate. Also  $p \ne 0$ , since otherwise  $B, O, D$  colinear would force  $B, C \equiv O, D$  colinear contradicting convexity. Finally,  $\overline{a}b = a\overline{b}$  would require  $A, B, O$ , and thus  $A, B, C$  colinear, contradiction again. Thus  $a\overline{b} + b\overline{a} = 0$ , so  $\angle{AOB}$  is a right angle. All algebra is reversible, so this is an if and only if statement.

