The roots of  $x^n+1$  are rotated versions of the roots of  $x^n-1$  by  $\tfrac{\pi}{n}$ . Note that the condition implies for some integer  $k$ , there exists an integer  $j$  such that  $e^{(2k)\pi ik/n}$  and  $e^{(2j+1)\pi i/n}$  are points at  $e^{4\pi i/6}$  and  $e^{2\pi i/6}$ , respectively. Therefore:
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{2k}{n} = \dfrac{4}{6} \quad \text{and} \quad \dfrac{2j+1}{n} = \dfrac{2}{6} \implies n = 3k = 6j+3.
\end{align*}
Thus,  $n \equiv 3 \pmod 6$ . Thus, the desired answer is  $\left\lfloor \dfrac{2012+3}{6} \right\rfloor = \boxed{335.}$

