&= 3(\cos(\theta)+i\sin(\theta))-\dfrac{1}{3}(\cos(\theta)-i\sin(\theta)) 
&= \dfrac{8}{3}\cos(\theta)+\dfrac{10}{3}i\sin(\theta) 
\end{align*}
which we note is an ellipse (Remark 8.1). Now, this is an ellipse with semi-major and semi-minor axis of  $\dfrac{10}{3}$  and  $\dfrac{8}{3}$  respectively. Thus, our answer is  $\boxed{\dfrac{80}{9}\pi}$ .

***Remark.***This can be proven by noting that in Cartesian coordinates, this is the set of all  $(x,y)$  where  $(x,y) = (\frac{8}{3}\cos(\theta),\frac{10}{3}\sin(\theta))$ . We can now note that this is equivalent to  $\dfrac{9}{64}x^2+\dfrac{9}{100}y^2 = 1$  which is clearly an ellipse.

Note that if  $w = 3\cos(\theta) + 3i \sin(\theta)$ , then
\begin{align*}
