Consider first the bishop. By Linearity of Expectation, the expected number of squares the bishop could land on can be written as
\begin{align*}
\tfrac{1}{4} \cdot \mathbb{E}[\# \text{ of Squares}] = 1\cdot p(1-p) + 2\cdot p^2 (1-p) + 3\cdot p^3(1-p) + \cdots
\end{align*}
Note that this is a arithmetico-geometrico sequence. Thus,
\begin{align*}
S &= 1\cdot p(1-p) + 2\cdot p^2 (1-p) + 3\cdot p^3(1-p) + \cdots 
Sp &= 1\cdot p^2(1-p) + 2\cdot p^3 (1-p) + 3\cdot p^4 (1-p) + \cdots 
S(1-p) &= 1 \cdot p(1-p) + 1 \cdot p^2(1-p) + 1 \cdot p^3(1-p) + \cdots 
S &= 1 \cdot p + 1 \cdot p^2 + 1 \cdot p^3 + \cdots.
\end{align*}
This is clearly a geometric sequence which can be calculated as  $\tfrac{p}{1-p}$ . Then, note that the expected number of squares a knight could land is  $8 \cdot p$  as there are  $8$  possible squares and the probability the meteor doesn't land on the square is  $p$ . Thus,
