<blockquote>[confidence booster](http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?f=132&t=264024) (I remembered this right after writing the problem)</blockquote>Based on the close "discrete relative" discussed in the linked thread, here are two general solutions (if you want to understand them better, first work out the discrete analogs)...

Let  $N=2012$  and  $M=\frac{1}{2011}$ ; we want the probability that  $x_{i+1} - x_i \le M$  for  $i=1,2,\ldots,N-1$ .

<details><summary>Official PIE Solution</summary>We claim that the probability  $x_{i+1} - x_i > M$  for all  $i\in S$  (where  $S$  is a fixed set of indices; call this an *$S$ -violating*set of points/reals) is  $\max(0,1-M|S|)^N$ . (*) Indeed, in the nontrivial case  $1-M|S|>0$ , each  $S$ -violating set corresponds to a unique set of  $N$  points from the interval  $[0,1-M|S|]$  (and vice versa): for each  $i\in S$ , we simply ``remove'' the segment  $[x_i,x_i+M)$  from the interval  $[x_i,x_{i+1})$  (or for the other direction, we ``insert'' the segment  $[x'_i,x'_i+M)$  at the beginning of the interval  $[x'_i,x'_{i+1})$ ).

By (*) and PIE, the desired probability is (using properties of finite differences and  $\prod_{j=0}^{N-1}((1-kM)-(1-jM))=0$ )
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-1)^k\binom{N-1}{k}\max(0,1-kM)^N 
&= \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-1)^k\binom{N-1}{k}(1-kM)^N 
&= \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-1)^k\binom{N-1}{k}(1-kM)^{N-1}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}(1-jM) 
&= \sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-1)^k\binom{N-1}{k}(-M)^{N-1}k^{N-1}\left(N-M\frac{N(N-1)}{2}\right) 
&= M^{N-1}\frac{N}{2}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-1)^{N-1-k}\binom{N-1}{k}k^{N-1} 
&= M^{N-1}\frac{N}{2}(N-1)!\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(-1)^{N-1-k}\binom{N-1}{k}\binom{k}{N-1} 
&= M^{N-1}\frac{N}{2}(N-1)! = \frac{1006\cdot2010!}{2011^{2010}}.
\end{align*}Thus our answer is
\begin{align*}
m+n=1006\cdot2010!+2011^{2010}
&\equiv11^{2010} 
&\equiv\binom{2010}{2}10^2+\binom{2010}{1}10+1\equiv601\pmod{1000}.
\end{align*}</details>

<details><summary>Official 'Continuous' Generating Function Solution</summary>Equivalently, we want the probability that  $y_1,y_2,\ldots,y_{N-1}\le M$  for a random solution in nonnegative integers to  $y_0+y_1+\cdots+y_N=1$ , or
\[\frac{[x^1](\int_0^M x^t\; dt)^{N-1}(\int_0^\infty x^t\; dt)^2}{[x^1](\int_0^\infty x^t\; dt)^{N+1}}.\]But  $\int_0^M x^t\; dt = \frac{x^M - 1}{\ln{x}}$  and for positive integers  $L$ ,  $(-\ln{x})^{-L} = \frac{1}{(L-1)!}\int_0^\infty x^t t^{L-1}\; dt$  (note that this is the [gamma function](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamma_function) when  $x=e^{-1}$ , and for  $L=1$  we have  $\int_0^\infty x^t\; dt = (-\ln{x})^{-1}$ ). Now by ``convolution,'' we find
\begin{align*}
[x^1](\int_0^M x^t\; dt)^{N-1}(\int_0^\infty x^t\; dt)^2
&=[x^1](1-x^M)^{N-1}(-\ln{x})^{-(N-1)}(-\ln{x})^{-2} 
&=[x^1](1-x^M)^{N-1}\frac{1}{N!}\int_0^\infty x^t t^N\; dt 
&=\frac{1}{N!}\sum_{0\le k\le N-1,\frac{1}{M}}(-1)^k\binom{N-1}{k}(1-kM)^N
\end{align*}and
\[
[x^1](\int_0^\infty x^t\; dt)^{N+1}
=[x^1](-\ln{x})^{-(N+1)}
=[x^1]\frac{1}{N!}\int_0^\infty x^t t^N\; dt
=\frac{1}{N!}.
\]We finish in the same way as the previous solution.</details>

