Very beautiful problem, i liked it a lot. And very easy as for P3 (almost 200 people got a perfect score!)

Let  $A$  be that set. Let  $k$  denote the number of composite numbers in  $A$  and  $k_1, \dots, k_k$  be those numbers. WLOG  $1\notin A$  and  $p\mid k_i\implies p\notin A$  (in other cases problem is trivial). There are  $m-((m+1)-k)=k-1$  primes not in  $A$ . Let  $p_1, \dots, p_{k-1}$  denote them. We have  $$ \begin{cases}
    k_1=p_1^{a_{1, 1}}p_2^{a_{1, 2}}\cdot...\cdot p_{k-1}^{a_{1, k-1}},
    k_2=p_1^{a_{2, 1}}p_2^{a_{2, 2}}\cdot...\cdot p_{k-1}^{a_{2, k-1}},
    \dots,
    k_k=p_1^{a_{k, 1}}p_2^{a_{k, 2}}\cdot...\cdot p_{k-1}^{a_{k, k-1}}
\end{cases} $$  for some non-negative  $a_{i, j}$ . We will show that there exists index  $i$  such that  $k_i\mid \prod_{j=1, j\ne i}^kk_j$ . For contradiction, assume that for every such  $i$  there exists  $j$  such that  $a_{i, j}>\sum_{t=1, t\ne i}^ka_{t, j}$ . But there are  $k-1$  such indexes, so one  $i$  has to appear twice. So there exist  $j\ne u$  such that  $$ \begin{cases}
    a_{i, j}>\sum_{t=1, t\ne i}^ka_{t, j},
    a_{i, u}>\sum_{t=1, t\ne i}^ka_{t, u}.
\end{cases} $$  Adding these inequalities gives an easy contradiction and we are done.  $\hfill \blacksquare$

[asy]
import olympiad;
real r= 15*sqrt(3)/(16+2*sqrt(19)), y=15*sqrt(3/2-sqrt(36/19))/(16+2*sqrt(19));
