solution_md:
Neat problem!

First note that obviously two of the three  $a,b,c$  cannot be negative, and if  $a,b,c<0$ , then  $a,b,c>0$  satisfies the condition as well. Thus, we may assume at least two of  $a,b,c$  are nonnegative.

Note that the given condition gives us that  $c=\frac{a+b}{ab-1}.$  This begs for the following trig substitution:  $a=\tan(A), b=\tan(B), c=-\tan(A+B)=\tan(\pi-A-B),$  where  $A,B \in [0,\pi/2)$  such that  $a,b\ge0$  as per the assumption stated above.**<span style="color:#f00">Lemma:</span>**  $\cos(A)\cos(B)\cos(C)\le \frac{1}{8}.$ *Proof.* Note that if  $A+B<\pi/2,$  then  $\cos(C)<0,$  so the claim is trivially proven. Thus assume  $A+B>\pi/2$  so  $C\in [0,\pi/2),$  which implies  $\cos(A),\cos(B),\cos(C)>0.$  Then by AM-GM  $$ \cos(A)\cos(B)\cos(C)\le \left(\frac{\cos(A)+\cos(B)+\cos(C)}{3}\right)^3. $$  Moreover, since  $\cos(x)$  is concave on the interval  $[0,\pi/2),$  then by Jensen's inequality,  $$ \frac{\cos(A)+\cos(B)+\cos(C)}{3}\le \cos\left(\frac{A+B+C}{3}\right)=\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)=\frac{1}{2}, $$  so we have  $$ \cos(A)\cos(B)\cos(C)\le \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3=\frac{1}{8}, $$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**    $\cos(A)^2+\cos(B)^2+\cos(C)^2\ge \frac{3}{4}$ *Proof.* From our lemma, we have that  $$ \cos(A)\cos(B)\cos(C)\le \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3=\frac{1}{8}, $$  Adding, multiplying, subtracting, rearranging and simplifying gives that
\begin{align*}
& 1-2\cos(A)\cos(B)\cos(C)\ge\frac{3}{4} 
\iff & 1-2\cos(A)\cos(B)(\sin(A)\sin(B)-\cos(A)\cos(B))\ge\frac{3}{4} 
\iff & (1-\cos^2(A))(1-\cos^2(B)) + (\cos^2(A)+\cos^2(B)+\cos^2(A)\cos^2(B))-2\cos(A)\cos(B)\sin(A)\sin(B)\ge\frac{3}{4} 
\iff & \cos^2(A)+\cos^2(B)+(\sin^2(A)\sin^2(B)-2\cos(A)\cos(B)\sin(A)\sin(B)+\cos^2(A)\cos^2(B))\ge\frac{3}{4} 
\iff & \cos^2(A)+\cos^2(B)+(\sin(A)\sin(B)-\cos(A)\cos(B))^2\ge \frac{3}{4} 
\iff & \cos^2(A)+\cos^2(B)+\cos^2(C)\ge\frac{3}{4},
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Now note that  $\cos^2(x)=\frac{1}{\tan^2(x)+1},$  so the proven claim is equivalent to   $$ \frac{1}{(\tan(A))^2+1}+\frac{1}{(\tan(B))^2+1}+\frac{1}{(\tan(C))^2+1}\ge \frac{3}{4}, $$  Substituting for  $a,b,c$  yields the desired  $$ \frac{1}{a^2+1}+\frac{1}{b^2+1}+\frac{1}{c^2+1}\ge \frac{3}{4} \quad\quad\quad\square $$

