Assume the order  $1<d_1\le d_2\le \ldots\le d_{12}<12.$  Note that if we find  $d_{i}^2+d_{i+1}^2> d_{i+2}^2$ , we are done as  $d_i, d_{i+1}$  and  $d_{i+2}$  indeed satisfy the conditions for being the side lengths of an acute triangle, i.e,
\begin{align*}
d_{i}+d_{i+1} \ge \sqrt{(d_i +d_{i+1})^2-2d_id_{i+1}}&>d_{i+2}
d_{i+1}+d_{i+2} &>d_i
d_{i+2}+d_i &>d_{i+1}.
\end{align*}
Suppose not, then  $d_{i}^2+d_{i+1}^2\le d_{i+2}$  for each  $1\le i\le 10$ . Inductively we arrive at,
\begin{align*}
2 &\le  d_1^2+d_2^2\le d_3^2
3 &\le d_2^2+d_3^2 \le d_4^2
5 &\le d_3^2+d_4^2\le d_5^2
&\vdotswithin{\le}
144 &\le d_{10}^2+d_{11}^2\le d_{12}^2.
\end{align*}
But by hypothesis we know  $d_{12}<12$  giving us a contradiction. Therefore, there exists some  $i$  for which  $d_i, d_{i+1}$  and  $d_{i+2}$  are the lengths of an acute triangle.  $\square$

