Let  $\sigma$  be the permutation of the integers from 0 through  $p-1$  inclusive such that  $\sigma(i) \equiv \pi(i) \pmod{p}$  for each  $i$  with  $0 \leq i < p$ . (The distinction between that  $\sigma$  and  $\pi$  is that  $\sigma$  is a permutation of integers, and not residue classes.) Consider the product  $\prod_{0 \leq i < j < p} \frac{\sigma(j) - \sigma(i)}{j-i}$ . Its absolute value is clearly 1, and its sign is  $(-1)^k$ , where  $k$  is the number of pairs  $(i,j)$  with  $i < j$  and  $\sigma(i) > \sigma(j)$ .  $\sigma$  is an even permutation if and only if  $k$  is even. Since  $1 \not \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$ , we have that \[ \prod_{0 \leq i < j < p} \frac{\sigma(j) - \sigma(i)}{j-i} = 1 \] if and only if \[ \prod_{0 \leq i < j < p} \frac{\sigma(j) - \sigma(i)}{j-i} \equiv 1 \pmod{p}. \]
Now, 
\begin{align*}
\prod_{0 \leq i < j < p} \frac{\sigma(j) - \sigma(i)}{j-i} 
&\equiv \prod_{0 \leq i < j < p} \frac{j^3 - i^3}{j-i} \equiv \prod_{0 \leq i < j < p} (j^2 + ij +i^2) 
&\equiv \prod_{j=1}^{p-1} j^2 \prod_{1 \leq i < j < p} (j^2 + ij + i^2) \equiv (p-1)!^2 \prod_{1  \leq i < j < p} i^2 \prod_{1 \leq i < j < p} (1 + (j/i) + (j/i)^2) 
&\equiv  \prod_{1  \leq i < j < p} i^2 \prod_{1 \leq i < j < p} (1 + (j/i) + (j/i)^2).
\end{align*}
We compute these two products separately. 
\begin{align*}
\prod_{1  \leq i < j < p} i^2 
&\equiv \prod_{i=1}^{p-1} (i^2)^{p-1-i} \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{p-1} i^{2i} \right)^{-1} 
&\equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2i} \prod_{i=\frac{p+1}{2}}^{p-1} i^{2i} \right)^{-1} 
& \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2i} \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} (p-i)^{2p - 2i} \right)^{-1} \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2i} \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2p - 2i} \right)^{-1} 
& \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2i+2p-2i} \right)^{-1} \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2p} \right)^{-1} 
& \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i^{2} \right)^{-1} \equiv \left( \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i \prod_{i=\frac{p+1}{2}}^{p-1} (p-i) \right)^{-1} 
&\equiv \left( (-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \prod_{i=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} i \prod_{i=\frac{p+1}{2}}^{p-1} i \right)^{-1} 
&\equiv \left( (p-1)! (-1)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \right)^{-1} \equiv (-1)^{\frac{p+1}{2}} \pmod{p}. 
\end{align*}
We claim that the product  $\prod_{1 \leq i < j < p} (1 + (j/i) + (j/i)^2)$  is congruent to 1 mod  $p$ . This is easy to check for  $p=3$ , so suppose  $p>3$ . We note that each  $j/i$  (i.e.,  $j$  multiplied by the inverse of  $i$  modulo  $p$ ) with  $0 \leq i < j < p$  is some residue  $k$  between 2 and  $p-1$  inclusive. Furthermore, for each residue  $k$  between 2 and  $p-1$  inclusive, there are exactly  $p-1$  pairs  $(i,j)$  with  $j/i \equiv k \pmod{p}$ . Additionally, of these pairs, exactly half satisfy  $i<j$ , since  $(i,j)$  works if and only if  $(p-i, p-j)$  works. Thus, \[ \prod_{1 \leq i < j < p} (1 + (j/i) + (j/i)^2) = \prod_{k=2}^{p-1} (1 + k + k^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}. \]

Now note that for any  $x$ ,  $1 + (p-1 - x) + (p-1-x)^2 \equiv 1 + x + x^2 \pmod{p}$ , so we have
\begin{align*}
\prod_{k=2}^{p-1} (1 + k + k^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}
&= (1 + \frac{p-1}{2} + (\frac{p-1}{2})^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} (1 + (p-2) + (p-2)^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \prod_{k=2}^{\frac{p-3}{2}} (1+k+k^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \prod_{k=\frac{p+1}{2}}^{p-3} (1 + k + k^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} 
&\equiv (\frac{3}{4})^{\frac{p-1}{2}} 3^{\frac{p-1}{2}}  \prod_{k=2}^{\frac{p-3}{2}} (1+k+k^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}  \prod_{k=2}^{\frac{p-3}{2}} (1 + (p-1-k) + (p-1-k)^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}} 
&\equiv 3^{p-1} 4^{-\frac{p-1}{2}} \prod_{k=2}^{\frac{p-3}{2}} \left( (1+k+k^2)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\right)^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{p}.
\end{align*}

Thus, our original product is congruent to  $(-1)^{\frac{p+1}{2}}$  mod  $p$ , which is 1 if and only if  $p \equiv 3 \pmod{4}$ , which was what we wanted.

