For  $n$  a positive integer, we have (using integration by parts for the first term of the integrand)
\begin{align*}
I_n = \int_{0}^{\alpha}\left(xe^{-nx}+\alpha x^{n-1}\right) \ dx &=- \frac{\alpha e^{-n \alpha}}{n}+\frac{1}{n}\int_{0}^{\alpha}e^{-nx} \ dx+\frac{\alpha ^{n+1}}{n}  &=- \frac{\alpha e^{-n \alpha}}{n}-\frac{e^{-n\alpha}-1}{n^2}+\frac{\alpha ^{n+1}}{n}= \frac{1}{n^2}+\frac{\alpha ^{n+1}}{n}-\frac{\alpha e^{-n \alpha}}{n}-\frac{e^{-n \alpha}}{n^2}.  \end{align*}
Therefore,  $n^2 I_n =1+n \alpha ^{n+1}-n \alpha e^{-na}-e^{-n \alpha} \rightarrow \boxed{1}$  as  $n \rightarrow \infty.$ From an examination of the graphs of  $|x|$  and  $e^{-x}$  it is clear that  $0.5 < \alpha < 0.6$  and so it is easy to show that  $n \alpha ^{n+1} \rightarrow 0$  as  $n \rightarrow \infty.$   The limits above are obvious or else follow from L'Hospital's rule.

