**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>** Let  $C'$  be the reflection of  $C$  over the angle bisector of  $\angle ABC$ . Then,  $BPCC'$  is cyclic.
*Proof.*Indeed, \begin{align*}
\measuredangle PCC'&=\measuredangle  ACC'+\measuredangle PCA=\frac{\measuredangle B+\measuredangle C}{2}-\frac{\measuredangle B+\measuredangle C}{4}&=\frac{\measuredangle B+\measuredangle C}{4}=\measuredangle PBA=\measuredangle PBC'.\,\square
\end{align*}

Let  $Q'$  be image of  $P$  of inversion at  $B$  with radius  $\sqrt{ac}$  followed by the reflection over the angle bisector of  $\angle ABC$ . Thus,  $Q'B$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle CQ'A$  and  $APCQ'$  is cyclic quadrilateral. Now, all we need is to show that  $P,L,Q'$  are collinear.
By ratio lemma,\begin{align*}
\frac{AL}{LC}=\frac{AB}{BC}=\frac{AB}{BC}\cdot \frac{\sin{\angle ABP}}{\sin{\angle PBC}}\cdot \frac{\sin{\angle ABQ'}}{\sin{\angle Q'BC}}=\frac{AP}{PC}\cdot \frac{AQ'}{Q'C},
\end{align*}we are done.  $\blacksquare$

