For a point  $X$  and a circle  $\Omega$ , let  $\mathcal{P}(X, \Omega)$  be the power of  $P$  WRT  $\Omega.$  Let  $M, N$  be the projections of  $P$  onto  $AC, AB$ , respectively, and denote  $\Gamma \equiv \odot (ABC), \omega \equiv \odot (ADE).$  We will prove that as  $P$  varies,  $\omega$  always passes through  $H$ , the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC.$ Define the function  $f : \mathbb{R} \mapsto \mathbb{R}^2$  by  $f(X) := \mathcal{P}(X, \omega) - \mathcal{P}(X, \Gamma).$  It is straightforward to check that  $f$  is linear. Then since  $H = \tfrac{1}{\tan A \tan B \tan C}\left(\tan A : \tan B : \tan C\right)$  are the normalized barycentric coordinates of  $H$ , we obtain 
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{P}(H, \omega) - \mathcal{P}(H, \Gamma) = f(H) &= \frac{\tan A \cdot f(A) + \tan B \cdot f(B) + \tan C \cdot f(C)}{\tan A \tan B \tan C}  
\implies \tan A \tan B \tan C\left(\mathcal{P}(H, \omega) - \mathcal{P}(H, \Gamma)\right) &= \tan B \cdot BD \cdot BA + \tan C \cdot CE \cdot CA  
&= \frac{PN}{BN} \cdot BD \cdot BA + \frac{PM}{CM} \cdot CE \cdot CA  
&= 2 \cdot PN \cdot BA + 2 \cdot PM \cdot CA  
&= 4[ABP] + 4[ACP] = 4[ABC],
\end{align*}
which is fixed. It follows that  $\mathcal{P}(H, \omega)$  is fixed as well. Thus, it suffices to show that  $\mathcal{P}(H, \omega) = 0$  for one choice of  $P.$ When  $P$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ , we see that  $E, D$  are the projections of  $B, C$  onto  $\overline{AC}, \overline{AB}$ , respectively. Thus,  $\omega$  is in fact the circle of diameter  $\overline{AH}$ , implying that  $\mathcal{P}(H, \omega) = 0.$  It follows that  $\mathcal{P}(H, \omega) = 0$  for any  $P$ , i.e.  $H \in \omega.$   $\square$

