Define  $f: \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$  by  $f(X) = (X, \odot (RSP)) - (X, \odot (RSQ))$  where  $(X, \omega)$  denotes the power of  $X$  with respect to circle  $\omega$ .  $f$  is linear, so
\begin{align*}
(Q, \odot(RSP)) - (Q, \odot(RSQ)) = f(Q)  &= f(A) \cdot \frac{QC}{AC} + f(C) \cdot \frac{AQ}{AC} &= (AP^2 - AQ^2)\frac{QC}{AC} + (CS \cdot CR - CQ^2) \frac{AQ}{AC} &= 0
\end{align*}
since  $AP = AQ$  and  $CQ^2 = CS \cdot CR$  by PoP. Thus  $(Q, \odot(RSP)) = (Q, \odot(RSQ)) = 0$ , so  $Q$  lies on  $\odot(RSP)$ , as desired.

