<details><summary>Solution</summary>We are going to use barycentric coordinates on  $\triangle ABC$ . Let  $A=(1,0,0)$ ,  $B=(0,1,0)$ ,  $C=(0,0,1)$  and  $a=BC$ ,  $b=CA$ ,  $c=AB$ . The condition  $AP=AQ$  is equivalent to letting
\begin{align*}
P&=\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c},\frac{bt}{b+c},0\right)
Q&=\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c},0,\frac{ct}{b+c}\right).
\end{align*}
Let  $R=(0,r,1-r)$ ,  $S=(0,s,1-s)$  so
\begin{align*}
BR&=(1-r)a
CR&=ra
BS&=(1-s)a
CS&=sa.
\end{align*}
The angle conditions are equivalent to  $\triangle BRP\sim\triangle BPS$ ,  $\triangle CSQ\sim\triangle CQR$  so  $BS\cdot BR=BP^2,CR\cdot CS=CQ^2$ . It follows that
\begin{align*}
(1-r)(1-s)a^2&=\left(c-\frac{bct}{b+c}\right)^2
rsa^2&=\left(b-\frac{bct}{b+c}\right)^2
\end{align*}
The equation for  $(PQR)$  is  $a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy=u_1x+v_1y+w_1z$  where
\begin{align*}
c^2\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)&=u_1\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)+v_1\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\tag{1}
b^2\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)&=u_1\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)+w_1\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\tag{2}
a^2r(1-r)&=v_1r+w_1(1-r).
\end{align*}
The equation for  $(PQS)$  is  $a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy=u_2x+v_2y+w_2z$  where
\begin{align*}
c^2\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)&=u_2\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)+v_2\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)
b^2\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)&=u_2\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)+w_2\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)
a^2s(1-s)&=v_2s+w_2(1-s).
\end{align*}
It suffices to prove that  $(u_1,v_1,w_1)=(u_2,v_2,w_2)$ . Assuming  $v_1=v_2=v$  and  $w_1=w_2=w$ , we get
\begin{align*}
a^2r(1-r)&=vr+w(1-r)
a^2s(1-s)&=vs+w(1-s)
\implies v&=a^2(1-r)(1-s)=\left(c-\frac{bct}{b+c}\right)^2
w&=a^2rs=\left(b-\frac{bct}{b+c}\right)^2.
\end{align*} $\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\text{(1)}-\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\text{(2)}$  yields
\begin{align*}
&c^2\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)-b^2\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)
=\text{ }&v\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)-w\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)
=\text{ }&c^2\left(\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)^2\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)-b^2\left(\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{bt}{b+c}\right)\left(1-\frac{ct}{b+c}\right)^2
\end{align*}
which is valid. Thus we have  $(u,v,w)=(u_1,v_1,w_1)=(u_2,v_2,w_2)$ .  $\square$</details>

